      "writtenOn": "2021-10-28T14:29:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah good point, I have mixed feelings about it: the documentation does not give a strong guideline[1]. Chosen is used for system or subsystem config (flash, sram, console...), it could certainly be used here, but then we\u0027d have to have a node label to point to anyway, so I feel like this would only add a bunch of redundant \"chosen { cros,kblight \u003d \u0026kblight; };\", which could be useful if we had some other reason to have a generic node label for the named pwm (I mean, not \"kblight\"), but we don\u0027t with the named-pwm entries.\n\nSimilar story with aliases, which do kinda the same thing but are mostly used to have portable samples so far.\n\nIn practice I think either would only add more redundancy. The other valid alternative, and potential evolution of this if we need more flexibility, would be to add a node, compatible and binding, and use that to find the device, though it seems unnecessary at this stage since the phandle is all that\u0027s needed by the driver.\n\nSo I think it\u0027s fine to use the node label for now, and then convert this to a normal device node once we\u0027ll get to the point of writing native drivers -- but if you feel strongly about it I\u0027m happy to switch it to a chosen or alias instead.\n\n[1] https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/guides/dts/intro.html#aliases-and-chosen-nodes",
      "parentUuid": "9276033f_f83465ca",
